CHAPTER 1
Civil Procedure and Why We Study It
Suppose you receive a letter from the local courthouse. It informs you that a man has accused you of hitting him with your car and is now suing you over the resulting injuries. In all, he is demanding $200,000. Such a lawsuit is called a civil action. In a civil action, a plaintiff(the person suing) seeks relief (usually a payment of money) against a defendant (the person being sued). This action is called “civil” not because it is conducted politely (although it should be that, too), but to distinguish it from a criminal action, in which the government prosecutes a person for committing a crime.

Now suppose that in fact you never injured this person. You don’t even have a car! The letter indicates that you are to come to the local courthouse the following week. If you don’t appear, the letter says, you are assumed to admit to hitting the man and will have to pay the money he demands. When you and the plaintiff (together, the parties) come to court, the judge ignores your protestations of innocence and tells the parties that the outcome of the case will be decided by a coin flip: heads, you win, and tails, the plaintiff wins. By a stroke of luck, you win the coin flip and don’t have to pay for the man’s injuries.

Is this coin flip a good method for resolving this courtroom dispute? Your intuition surely screams, “No!” And you feel this way even though the outcome in your case was correct because you didn’t, in fact, hit the plaintiff. The procedure for reaching that outcome was arbitrary, and nothing about it was designed to reach the right decision by considering the facts or the law. The proceeding was substantively fair—the objectively correct outcome was reached—but it was procedurally unfair, since the process by which the court reached the outcome failed to give the parties the opportunity to present evidence or make any arguments. Moreover, the coin flip was just as likely to go the other way, and if so you would have had to pay for injuries you did not cause.

4
Happily, this scenario is unrealistic, but it shows how we instinctively place value on fair and sensible processes for deciding disputes. Our intuition tells us that the procedure by which we reach legal outcomes must be fair and involve consideration of the merits of the case—in other words, consideration of the facts giving rise to the dispute and the laws governing the parties’ conduct that led to the dispute. Of course, we also care whether a dispute ends with the right outcome—we want to see that the winner is the person who should have won. But as the coin flip example reminds us, merely obtaining the right outcome is not enough. The process that gives us the outcome must be fair.

This principle is not just an intuition: it is enshrined in the United States Constitution. A court cannot enter a judgment depriving a person of life, liberty, or property without “due process of law,” and these words appear not once but twice in the Constitution—in the Fifth Amendment, which constrains the federal government, and the Fourteenth Amendment, which constrains the states.

The Constitution requires “due process.” But how much process is due? Does a person whose legal claims are at stake have a right to a hearing before the court enters a judgment on those claims? Does a person in a civil action have a right to have a literal “day in court”—a trial in which they present arguments and question witnesses? And if they do get a trial, do they get a jury? As we shall see in the chapters that follow, the answer to each of these questions is “sometimes.”

It is questions like these that civil procedure attempts to answer. Civil procedure is the set of practices and rules that define the process by which parties, lawyers, and judges resolve disputes in civil actions. We shall study many facets of civil practice, from before suit is filed, through the day-to-day of litigation, and up to and beyond final judgment by the court. At every step, we expect the court to provide the parties “due process.” And at every step, we again must ask, But how much process is due? This is a central theme of this book, and it has both a descriptive aspect—what procedures do courts have?—and a normative aspect—what procedures should courts have?

This book’s primary goal is to address the descriptive aspect (what the law is) while leaving open normative questions (what the law should be) for you to judge. We shall see that not all procedures can be judged as easily as the coin flip in the scenario above. It is easy to agree that we want fair process, but it is hard to define what makes a process fair. It is easy to demand that courts
