provide parties with a full airing of the law and evidence, but gathering evidence and researching the law is expensive; when a party has limited wealth, does insisting on more evidence and longer arguments make the process fairer, or less fair? And might there even be circumstances where a coin flip is fair? We will not see coin flips again in this book, but we will read a case where a federal judge orders the parties to play rock-paper-scissors. And maybe, in that case, rock-paper-scissors was exactly the process that was due.

