Procedure Is Power
There is a second theme to this book. It is one thing to describe procedure—to know what the law of procedure is—and to evaluate procedure—to judge what it should be. It is another thing to use it. You did not come to law school to learn the law. You came to law school to become a lawyer. And a lawyer’s value is not that the lawyer knows the law. It is that the lawyer knows how to use the law. To win.

Let’s say you are a lawyer, and a person comes to you and asks you to represent them, because something happened to them and they need to go to court. How do you win? You can’t change what happened. You can’t change the laws that, at the time of the events giving rise to the dispute, defined what each party should and shouldn’t have done.

What is under your control? What can you change? You can control when and where the case is heard. You can control what claims and arguments you make to the judge. You can control how much and what kinds of evidence you collect. You can control how the issues are presented to the judge or jury. You can control whether to settle the case or press on with litigation.

In short, procedure is power. Command of procedure is a source of power for lawyers. And it should therefore come as no surprise that any trial lawyer or judge will tell you that most of what they do is procedure.

Of course, prudent clients and their lawyers, long before they ever have to set foot in a courtroom, focus on other things. They focus on the substantive law and the conduct of the client and others: How do we comply with the law and therefore avoid lawsuits in the first place? How do we anticipate the actions of others and thereby avoid encounters that end badly? And do we consider the law to be fair, or should we advocate for legal change? But once a dispute has arisen, it is too late for any of this. Procedure rules the day.

But don’t take my word for it. Read for yourself.
